t you end up having in most real-world enterprise data scenarios is, the technical term for it is a giant freaking mess. You have a giant freaking mess in the data warehouse. You're writing tables from your extract and load process, you're querying them from these BI tools and none of it's governed, none of it's well managed. So we can see here there's some problems. There's no data lineage. That's a problem. The impact of this problem is that there's not a lot of trust in this data. Where did the data come from? How up to date is it? Is the data quality good or is it problematic? There's no data documentation. So it's hard to find the data sets that you actually care about. This is like a common problem that data analysts talk about in the enterprise. How do I find the data sets that I need to do my job? There's no deployment process. So frequently, you just develop in prod. You update the tables in prod. You update the BI dashboards in prod. It breaks. People find out that it's broken. They don't trust you or your data team anymore. Breaks, people find out that it's broken. They don't trust you or your data team anymore. There's no QA here. Your dashboards are broken. They have phony numbers. It's out of date again. Everything's wrong. And there's fragmented business logic because you're writing the same SQL over and over again in different dashboards and different operational tools and your ML and AI use cases. So you see inconsistent data and people don't know what is correct anymore. So dbt attempts to solve for this problem. And we do so using what we'll call like a transformation layer. And so the idea here is we're still gonna extract and load data into the data warehouse as before, but this time we're gonna extract and load data into what we'll call like the raw half of the data warehouse. From here, these blue lines represent a transformation pipeline. So this is dbt's job. You define your business logic in SQL, where you say this is a customer, this is an order, this is an ad campaign, and then you create these transform data sets that can then be queried from your, you know, BI, ML, and AI operational use cases. So it's a much cleaner separation where you're not defining your business logic in these sort of use case dashboard, et cetera, et cetera. Okay. Why is this better? Well, we can now version control our data transformations. So we can define our business logic in code. We can commit it to say a GitHub repository. And the upshot of this is that it's easy now to view and update your business logic over time as your business or organization evolves. You can visualize your data lineage. So you can see how data flows from a data source, like a system record, like Salesforce, for example, through your transformations into a BI tool. You can use CICD, which importantly helps you develop in dev as opposed to prod, which is where we should be developing. And importantly, you can rate automated testing on your transformations so you can find out if your data is in bad shape before a stakeholder like your CEO does. Generally, you don't want the CEO to find out that your data is broken before you know about it. Last, you can document your datasets. So you can reuse instead of rebuild these transformations, everyone can operate on top of the same customer's dataset or orders dataset rather than reinventing the real, sometimes you're incorrectly. I'm gonna give you a quick look at dbt in practice. For the most part, it's just SQL. So this is an example, we call it a dbt you know in practice for the most part it's just sql so this is an example we call it a dbt model this one's called stage github stars this is if you've ever been on github you can like star a repository this is a data set that's like tabulating how many stars we have on dbt's open source repository i want to show you a couple cool things about a dbt model for one in addition to just being regular SQL, you could also use what is called Jinja templating language. Here we're actually referring to a source dataset from SQL. This is how dbt derives its understanding of lineage of how data flows. We're transforming the GitHub Stargazer's source dataset into this staged representation of the data. You can write whatever SQL you want here. This gets executed against your cloud data warehouse. In this case, we're deduplicating people who star, then unstar, then that restart data set. So that's the typical dbt model. I just want to show you one more thing here. This is sort of visualization of your lineage graph, or we call it DAG, directed acyclic graph. So you can see there's data sources. In this case, you can actually see we use Fivetrain internally to load data from, this is Stripe data, so payments data. This flows through a whole bunch of different intermediate types of transformations. And finally, we output, in this case, a sort of dimensional model, we'd call it. So this is Stripe customers, and I know it's truncated, but Dimstripe customers. This is the dataset that we actually exposed to the business. Everything to the left of this is hidden from business users. They don't need to see it, they don't need to care about it, it's none of their business. What we tell them to do is go to Dimstripe customers if they have any questions at all about our customers from payment data in Stripe. We have tons and tons of metadata that gets generated, if they have any questions at all about our customers from pavement data in Stripe. So we have tons and tons of metadata that gets generated, either defined by dbt users or kind of generated by dbt as it runs, like was the data transformation successful? Is it a table or a view? How long did it take to build? Was it successful? All that kind of stuff. So we can visualize that for you. We can also expose it via API. This is the best way to run analytics at scale, is to have this well-governed type of system and document and expose the business.",
